/* ============================================================
   Maths exam questions, pulled from the A level topic PDFs

   The built-in chapter bank is deliberately gentle: it is there
   to check you followed the video. These are the real thing —
   358 Edexcel questions carrying 3354 marks, at the tariffs the
   paper actually sets, which is why a practice test built from
   them feels like a paper and one built from the bank does not.

   WHAT IS TRUSTWORTHY HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT

   The tariff, the question number, the topic and the page are
   exact: they are plain text in a normal font, and the tariff is
   summed from the printed part marks.

   The TEXT is best effort. A diagram is vector art, so only its
   stray labels reach the text layer; a fraction is laid out
   rather than encoded, so it arrives as a numerator above a
   denominator; and some papers embed a subsetted font with no
   ToUnicode map, which comes out shifted three letters along.
   `flags` says which of those applies:

     figure   the question refers to a figure that is not text
     layout   a fraction or similar has come apart over two lines
     font     part of the wording is in the broken encoding

   Which is why every question carries `pageFrom`/`pageTo`. The
   text is what you read; the page is what you look at when the
   text is not enough, rendered from the PDF itself. 145 of these
   need nothing else; the rest is why the button is there.

   The mark scheme is the topic's own scheme PDF, opened at the
   document rather than the question: these are compilations, so
   the same question number recurs, and mark-scheme pages are
   full of algebra that begins with a digit. Every automatic way
   of locating one question's scheme drifted, and a scheme
   attached to the wrong question is worse than one you have to
   find. `num` is that question's number in its source paper,
   which is what to look for.
   ============================================================ */

