 {"id":"alProbability-10","set":"alProbability","topic":"Probability","strand":"Stats","num":4,"marks":11,"pageFrom":96,"pageTo":96,"chapters":["ch:st5", "ch:st2c2"],"flags":[],"text":"A training agency awards a certificate to each student who passes a test while completing\na course.\nStudents failing the test will attempt the test again up to 3 more times, and, if they pass\nthe test, will be awarded a certificate.\nThe probability of passing the test at the first attempt is 0.7, but the probability of passing\nreduces by 0.2 at each attempt.\n(a) Complete the tree diagram below to show this information.\n0.7 Pass Pass Pass\n0.5\n............. 0.3 .............\nFail Fail\nPass\n.............\n............. Fail\n............. Fail (2)\nA student who completed the course is selected at random.\n(b) Find the probability that the student was awarded a certificate.\n(2 marks)\n(c) Given that the student was awarded a certificate, find the probability that the student\npassed on the first or second attempt.\n(3 marks)\nThe training agency decides to alter the test taken by the students while completing the\ncourse, but will not allow more than 2 attempts. The agency requires the probability of\npassing the test at the first attempt to be p, and the probability of passing the test at the\nsecond attempt to be ( p – 0.2). The percentage of students who complete the course and\nare awarded a certificate is to be 95%\n(d) Show that p satisfies the equation\np2 – 2.2p + 1.15 = 0\n(3 marks)\n(e) Hence find the value of p, giving your answer to 3 decimal places.\n(3 marks)"},
 {"id":"alProbability-11","set":"alProbability","topic":"Probability","strand":"Stats","num":4,"marks":9,"pageFrom":97,"pageTo":97,"chapters":["ch:st5", "ch:st2c2"],"flags":[],"text":"Events A and B are shown in the Venn diagram below\nwhere x, y, 0.10 and 0.32 are probabilities.\nA B\nx 0.10 y\n0.32\n(a) Find an expression in terms of x for\n(i) P(A)\n(ii) P(B | A)\n(3 marks)\n(b) Find an expression in terms of x and y for P( A ∪ B)\n(1 marks)\nGiven that P(A) = 2P(B)\n(c) find the value of x and the value of y\n(5 marks)"},
 {"id":"alProbability-12","set":"alProbability","topic":"Probability","strand":"Stats","num":5,"marks":10,"pageFrom":98,"pageTo":98,"chapters":["ch:st5", "ch:st2c2"],"flags":[],"text":"A group of 100 students are asked if they like folk music, rock music or soul music.\nAll students who like folk music also like rock music\nNo students like both rock music and soul music\n75 students do not like soul music\n12 students who like rock music do not like folk music\n30 students like folk music\n(a) Draw a Venn diagram to illustrate this information.\n(4 marks)\n(b) State two of these types of music that are mutually exclusive.\n(1 marks)\nFind the probability that a randomly chosen student\n(c) does not like folk music, rock music or soul music,\n(1 marks)\n(d) likes rock music,\n(1 marks)\n(e) likes folk music or soul music.\n(1 marks)\nGiven that a randomly chosen student likes rock music,\n(f) find the probability that he or she also likes folk music.\n(2 marks)"},
 {"id":"alProbability-13","set":"alProbability","topic":"Probability","strand":"Stats","num":7,"marks":9,"pageFrom":99,"pageTo":100,"chapters":["ch:st5", "ch:st2c2"],"flags":["layout"],"text":"In a large college, 3 of the students are male, 3 of the students are left handed and\n5 10\n1 of the male students are left handed.\n5\nA student is chosen at random.\n(a) Given that the student is left handed, find the probability that the student is male.\n(2 marks)\n(b) Given that the student is female, find the probability that she is left handed.\n(3 marks)\n(c) Find the probability that the randomly chosen student is male and right handed.\n(2 marks)\nTwo students are chosen at random.\n(d) Find the probability that one student is left handed and one is right handed.\n(2 marks)"}
